::: {.doc-subject}

Project a bipartite graph onto one of its partitions.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L24004-L24182){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.bipartite_projection(
    which=0,
    weighting="count",
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Bipartite projection](../assets/graphs/bipartite-projection.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="A bipartite graph with A, B, C, D on top and E, F below; E, in the accent color, is joined to A, B, C and F, in teal, to B, C, D. Projecting onto the top side joins two nodes when they share a neighbor below, weighted by how many they share: A-B and A-C share only E (accent), B-D and C-D share only F (teal), and B-C shares both, so it is a plain edge of weight 2." width="100%"}

A projection turns a two-mode network (e.g. people and the events they attend) into a
one-mode network of just one kind of node (people linked to people). Two nodes in the
chosen partition become connected if they share at least one neighbor in the other
partition, and the edge weight equals the number of shared neighbors. The partitions are
those returned by `bipartite_sets()`.

Projections are the standard way to analyze bipartite data with ordinary one-mode tools
(centrality, community detection, etc.). Typical examples:

- co-authorship networks from author-paper data
- gene-gene networks from gene-disease associations (genes linked by shared diseases)
- product similarity from customer purchase data

The result is a *new* graph; the original graph is left unchanged. The projection is always
undirected and weighted. Each node keeps its ID, label and attributes from the original
graph; the edges are new, so they get fresh IDs. Nodes of the chosen partition that share no
neighbor with any other node still appear, as isolated nodes. Note that projections can be
much denser than the original graph: a node in the other partition with *d* neighbors
creates up to *d(d - 1)/2* projected edges. On directed graphs edge direction is ignored,
both for the partitioning and for which nodes count as shared neighbors.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[which]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[0]{.dv}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Which partition to project onto: `0` (default) for the first set returned by
    `bipartite_sets()` or `1` for the second. In each connected component, partition `0`
    is the side containing the component's lowest node ID, so it's often clearest to
    look up the partition that holds a known node (see the examples). Any other value
    raises an error.

<code>[weighting]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[&quot;count&quot;]{.st}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   How projected edges are weighted, for nodes `u` and `v` with neighbor sets `N(u)`
    and `N(v)` in the other partition:

    - `"count"` (default): the number of shared neighbors, `|N(u) & N(v)|`
    - `"collaboration"`: Newman's collaboration weight, the sum over shared neighbors
      `n` of `1 / (deg(n) - 1)`, so a shared paper with many authors counts for less
    - `"jaccard"`: `|N(u) & N(v)| / |N(u) | N(v)|`
    - `"overlap"`: `|N(u) & N(v)| / min(|N(u)|, |N(v)|)`

    These match NetworkX's `weighted_projected_graph`,
    `collaboration_weighted_projected_graph` and `overlap_weighted_projected_graph`
    (with `jaccard=True` and `jaccard=False`).
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   A new undirected, weighted graph containing only the nodes of the chosen partition,
    with edges weighted as chosen by `weighting=`.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If the graph is not bipartite, `which=` is not `0` or `1`, or `weighting=` is
    unknown.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~biadjacency_matrix`) 

:   The same information as a matrix of rows and columns.

[](`~bipartite_sets`) 

:   The two sides of a bipartite graph.

[](`~robins_alexander_clustering`) 

:   How often the links between two sides close into squares.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

Projected onto the people, Ana and Ben are linked through the chess club, and Ben and
Cruz through the choir:

::: {#fb3392ad .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [("Ana", "Chess"), ("Ben", "Chess"), ("Ben", "Choir"),
     ("Cruz", "Choir")]
)
people = graph.bipartite_projection(which=0)

people.get_edge_df(labels=True).select("source", "target", "weight")
```

